2.)  Take a real-world operational system that can be observed (bank, supermarket)
and identify the elements associated with it in terms of variability, complexity,
interconnectedness.


Lets follow the supermarket example as that is my favorite.

Variability
- As we know it is impossible to predict how many customers will come into a store at any given time.
It is even more difficult to predict how many people who enter the store and actually buy something.
Profitable customers and people who are hanging out are two separate categories of people that enter stores.
Then, we have to think about how many people will go to each cashier lane. This can vary depending on a variety of factories such as
preference of a particular cashier, cashier speed, whether the cashier lane is limited to a certain amount of items, etc.

Complexity
- Combinatorial complexity
-- Supermarkets consist of many different parts such as suppliers, employees, customers,
 management, supply and demand, infrastructure, economics.

-Dynamic Complexity
-- Well we know that this is based on cause and effect. To me, this seems to work very closely with
and mimics supply and demand because if even one part of the system is moving faster,
 is demanding more, and is out of sync with other parts then the whole system will face change.
 An example of this with the supermarket is ordering stock. During Covid-19 the supermarket I worked at
 faced supply issues because the demand outweighed the supply that was in stock and suppliers faced a bottleneck
 in distribution which led to customer dissatisfaction and a loss in profit. Now this was a drastic and very unprecedented example,
 but I think it proves the point of the complexity and interconnectedness of the system.


 Interconnectedness
 Interconnectedness goes hand in hand with dynamic complexity because as mentioned previously the system depends
 on all parts working together and if one part is missing then the whole thing will be affected.